This wake's main new result: keeping only the half of the slots that the decoder attends to most, once it has seen the question, costs nothing on documents. It scored .610, the same as with all slots (1,500 questions, seed 42 only). The second seed is queued.

## Done this wake
- **Long-chunk question settled at both seeds (arm B).** The gain comes from putting the two answer passages in the same chunk:
  - Answer passages joined, all others in their own chunks, against ten separate chunks: +.073 at seed 42 and +.067 at seed 43 (n=1,500 each, same slot count). The gain is on "bridge" questions, where the answer needs facts from both passages.
  - One chunk holding all ten passages gains only +.014 at seed 43, and that is not significant. The "+.037" the campaign set out to explain is about half noise; the law now marks it.
  - Passage order inside a chunk does not matter at either seed. Joining the distractor passages is below the noise band at both seeds.
- **Slot pruning by decoder attention (arm E, seed 42)**, 1,500 document questions, half the slots kept (slot = one compressed vector):

  | which half is kept | judge accuracy ↑ | vs all slots (.610) |
  |---|---|---|
  | most-attended, over all pages | .610 | +.000 [−.009, +.010] |
  | most-attended, per chunk | .606 | −.004 |
  | every 2nd slot | .505 | −.105 |
  | first half of each chunk | .495 | −.115 |

  The selection needs one full pass over every slot first. So it saves decoder context, not storage.
- **Arm G (re-compress the picked page at twice the slots, instead of showing it raw):** wrote the option, 25 lines across the eval script and its launcher; the default path is unchanged. It passes a syntax check only. The real tests are queued behind it.
- **Verifier fixes:**
